    "content": "The Artemis program continues in April 2026 with the 10-day, crewed Artemis II lunar flyby, scheduled to launch on April 1, 2026, aimed at testing Orion’s life-support systems. Following this, Artemis III (mid-2027) plans to land astronauts on the moon, with subsequent missions (Artemis IV-V) scheduled annually from 2028. Artemis Mission Schedule (As of April 2026) Artemis I (Completed): Launched Nov. 16, 2022. Successful uncrewed test flight of SLS rocket and Orion spacecraft orbiting the Moon. Artemis II (Crewed Flyby): Scheduled launch April 1, 2026. Duration: ~10 days. Objective: First crewed flight of Orion to test systems beyond low Earth orbit. Mission Profile: Crew will fly around the Moon (free-return trajectory) and splash down in the Pacific Ocean. Artemis III (Crewed Lunar Landing): Targeted for mid-2027. Will use SpaceX or Blue Origin landers to land the first woman and first person of color on the South Pole. Artemis IV (Lunar Landing): Targeted for early 2028. Focused on bringing international crew to the Gateway station and the surface. Artemis V (Lunar Landing): Targeted for late 2028. Artemis II 10-Day Timeline Breakdown Launch Day: Liftoff from NASA's Kennedy Space Center (LC-39B), insertion into Earth orbit, and systems check. Translunar Injection: Orion performs a critical engine burn to leave Earth orbit for the Moon. Days 2-4: Journey to the Moon. Days 5-6: Lunar flyby, passing within ~4,100 miles of the lunar surface, using a \"slingshot\" gravity maneuver. Days 7-10: Return to Earth, atmospheric re-entry, and splashdown. Note: Launch dates are subject to change based on technical readiness, including evaluation of the Artemis I heat shield and Artemis II system testing.",
